# UNIVERSAL TYPESCRIPT STRICT RULES

## 1. MANDATORY TYPING RULES (STRICT COMPILATION)

### 1.1 Absolute Prohibition of `any`
- **Rule**: Explicit and implicit `any` are strictly prohibited.
- **Rationale**: The `any` type disables static type checking, masking programming errors and undermining the safety of the codebase.
- **Mandatory Practice**: For dynamic, unvalidated, or unknown data (e.g., API responses, parsed JSON, third-party libraries), use the `unknown` type. You must narrow the type to a specific contract using type guards, assertion functions, or schema validation before performing any operations on it.
- **Compilation Enforcement**: Enable `noImplicitAny` inside the compiler options to report errors on any variables or expressions with an implied `any` type.

```typescript
// INCORRECT / ANTIPATTERN
function processPayload(data: any) {
  console.log(data.userId); // Unchecked property access
  data.execute();          // Unchecked method call
}

// CORRECT / CLEAN
interface UserPayload {
  userId: string;
}

function isUserPayload(value: unknown): value is UserPayload {
  return (
    typeof value === "object" &&
    value !== null &&
    "userId" in value &&
    typeof (value as UserPayload).userId === "string"
  );
}

function processPayloadSafe(data: unknown): void {
  if (isUserPayload(data)) {
    console.log(data.userId); // Safe, type narrowed to UserPayload
  } else {
    throw new Error("Invalid payload structure");
  }
}
```

### 1.2 Explicit Return Types for Public Boundaries
- **Rule**: You must declare explicit return types on all exported functions, public class methods, and public API boundaries.
- **Rationale**: While local type inference is convenient, explicit return types at public boundaries serve as documentation, surface type errors faster when internal logic shifts, and significantly reduce compiler overhead by preventing the generation of deep, anonymous import paths in generated declaration files (`.d.ts`).
- **Compilation Enforcement**: Enable `declaration` and `isolatedDeclarations` to ensure all exports are annotated sufficiently to trivially generate declaration files.

```typescript
// INCORRECT / ANTIPATTERN
export function getUserSession(id: string) {
  return {
    id,
    active: true,
    roles: ["admin"]
  };
}

// CORRECT / CLEAN
export interface UserSession {
  id: string;
  active: boolean;
  roles: string[];
}

export function getUserSessionSafe(id: string): UserSession {
  return {
    id,
    active: true,
    roles: ["admin"]
  };
}
```

### 1.3 Total Prohibition of the Non-Null Assertion Operator (`!`)
- **Rule**: The non-null assertion operator (`!`) is strictly forbidden.
- **Rationale**: The `!` operator silences the compiler without inserting any runtime protection, exposing the application to crashes if a value is `null` or `undefined` at runtime.
- **Mandatory Practice**: Use optional chaining (`?.`), nullish coalescing (`??`), or explicit flow control blocks to check and handle the absence of a value safely.
- **Compilation Enforcement**: Enable `strictNullChecks` to force explicit checks for `null` and `undefined` types.

```typescript
// INCORRECT / ANTIPATTERN
function getProfilePicture(user?: { avatarUrl?: string }): string {
  return user!.avatarUrl!; // Crashes if user or avatarUrl is missing
}

// CORRECT / CLEAN
function getProfilePictureSafe(user?: { avatarUrl?: string }): string {
  if (!user || !user.avatarUrl) {
    return "https://example.com/default-avatar.png";
  }
  return user.avatarUrl;
}
```

### 1.4 Safe Type Casting and Assertion Boundaries
- **Rule**: Avoid unsafe type assertions (`as Type`). Under no circumstances use double assertions (`as unknown as Type`) unless working with third-party library typing bugs.
- **Rationale**: Type assertions bypass type-checking during compilation and do not emit runtime assertions, creating a gap between the declared type and actual runtime representation.
- **Mandatory Practice**: Rely on structural annotations (`: Type`) when initializing object literals. Apply user-defined type guards (`is`) or assertion functions (`asserts`) when interacting with dynamic runtime data.

```typescript
// INCORRECT / ANTIPATTERN
interface Book {
  title: string;
  pages: number;
}

// Creating an object literal with an assertion hides omitted properties
const myBook = {
  title: "Clean Code"
} as Book; // Compiles fine, but pages is undefined at runtime!

// CORRECT / CLEAN
// Enforce compile-time property validation via direct type annotation
const myBookSafe: Book = {
  title: "Clean Code",
  pages: 450 // Compiler forces inclusion of all required properties
};
```

